testing libstdoc
===============

The tests are registered with CTest and run with:

    cmake -S . -B build
    cmake --build build
    ctest --test-dir build --output-on-failure

Run a subset by name:

    ctest --test-dir build -R heap --output-on-failure


What is registered today
--------------------------

    index_output          golden output of index.c
    format_output         golden output of format.c
    error_handling        failure paths (self-checking)
    heap_checks           allocator, safe variants, leak counts
    heap_abort_overflow   safe size overflow terminates with 134
    heap_abort_oom        safe out of memory terminates with 134
    heap_abort_double_free
                          safe double free terminates with 134
    heap_abort_invalid_pointer
                          safe bad pointer terminates with 134
    index_cxx_output      the same golden output, from C++


The three kinds of test
-----------------------

Golden output
    The program prints, CompareOutput.cmake runs it and requires two
    things: an exit status of 0 and standard output identical to the
    golden file in test/.  A test that fails without printing anything
    is still reported, because the status is checked as well.

    Used by index_output, format_output and index_cxx_output.

Self-checking
    The program performs its own checks, prints "FAIL: ..." for each
    one that does not hold, and returns the number of failures as its
    exit status.  Zero is a pass, so no golden file is needed.

    Used by error_handling (test/errors.c) and heap_checks
    (test/heap.c).

Fatal paths
    A scenario that the library is documented to terminate with: the
    program names the scenario on the command line, does something
    invalid, and must end inside libstdoc with status 134.
    CheckFatal.cmake asserts exactly that status, so a scenario that
    exits cleanly, exits with a different status or crashes fails the
    test instead of passing unnoticed.

    Used by the four heap_abort_* tests (test/heap_abort.c).


Golden files
------------

test/expected-format.txt was produced from test/format.c by a
reference C library rather than by libstdoc itself, so the comparison
is a real check and not a snapshot of whatever the implementation
happens to do.  Regenerate it from the repository root with a hosted
compiler:

    cc -DSTDOC_REFERENCE -w test/format.c -o reference
    ./reference > test/expected-format.txt

test/expected-index.txt matches what index.c prints.  Regenerating a
golden file changes what the library is held to: review the diff
before committing it.


Rules a new test follows
------------------------

  * Freestanding tests compile assembly/start_aarch64.S into
    themselves, so linking never depends on the linker recovering a
    symbol from an archive, and link with -nostdlib -static -no-pie.
  * Every C test uses the full warning policy
    (-Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic -Werror) with -fno-builtin and
    -fno-stack-protector, unless it is one of the two legacy programs
    (index.c, index.cxx) that predate the policy.
  * A test that needs internals includes the internal header of that
    layer (io/stdoc_io.h or mem/stdoc_mem.h) by adding the directory
    to that target only.  errors.c does this on purpose: a stream
    bound to a bad descriptor cannot be obtained through the public
    interface, and its sticky-error behaviour is worth testing.
  * A test that checks a value carries its own verdict (exit status),
    so it needs no golden file.


The leak policy of the heap tests
---------------------------------

The allocator tests use small allocations only.  The arena is 4096
bytes by default, so anything a bug loses stays a few kilobytes at
most, and the accounting in mem/stdoc_mem.h turns any leak into a
failing check: after every scenario the live byte and block counts
must be back at zero.  The exhaustion scenario deliberately fills the
arena to prove that a full heap behaves - stdoc_malloc reports it,
printf falls back to its stack buffer, and releasing everything
restores the whole arena.

The tests do not depend on the arena size: they fill whatever heap
the library was built with.  Building with -DSTDOC_HEAP_SIZE=1024 and
running the same tests is a supported and useful variation.


What is not tested
------------------

  * Sanitizers and valgrind are not run: a "-nostdlib" freestanding
    program has no runtime for them.  Leak detection uses the
    library's own accounting instead.
  * Only the host that builds the project (aarch64 Linux) is
    exercised.  No other operating system or architecture is claimed
    to work without a toolchain and a successful build.
